finding: The rendered regression does not reject lost detail or reduced-contrast mip steps. At texture-minification.gpu.test.ts:55-60 it only upper-bounds detailBoundary's absolute contrast slope; no positive detail/content constraint is checked for hardware/manual captures (the texel assertions inspect only the separate nearest capture). Independently, in an isolated HEAD archive, returning vec4f(0.3,0.3,0.3,1) whenever settings alpha bit 1 is set makes both linear-mipmap paths uniformly grey and the entire test still passes, including the nearest assertions. Replacing those same paths with floor(lod+0.5) selection mixed 10% over constant grey also leaves a stepped image and the entire test passes. Landing this oracle permits the very regression it claims to hold to be hidden by blur or contrast loss. Add a positive detail/content constraint and controls that reject uniformly blurred and still-stepped outputs. The sampling fix itself passed independent Metal and llvmpipe comparisons; reverting only mip filtering made the regression red with the recorded 268.3296/271.7984 values.
